{
  "subject": "Electromagnetism",
  "parts": [
    {
      "label": "I.1",
      "asks": "The potential V(z) on the axis of the charged disc.",
      "hint": "Split the disc into thin rings of radius r and add up their potentials.",
      "steps": [
        "A ring of radius r and width dr holds dq = σ·2πr dr.",
        "Every point of the ring is √(r² + z²) from P, so dV = dq / (4πε₀√(r² + z²)).",
        "V(z) = (σ/2ε₀) ∫₀ᴿ r dr / √(r² + z²) = (σ/2ε₀)[√(r² + z²)]₀ᴿ."
      ],
      "answer": {
        "text": "V(z) = (σ/2ε₀)(√(R² + z²) − |z|)",
        "expression": "sigma/(2*eps0)*(sqrt(R^2+z^2)-abs(z))"
      },
      "uses": [],
      "followUps": [
        "Why add up rings instead of points?",
        "Where does the |z| come from?",
        "What happens to V far from the disc?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "label": "I.2",
      "asks": "The field E at the same point P.",
      "hint": "By symmetry only E_z survives; get it from the potential you found.",
      "steps": [
        "On the axis the field points along z, so E = −dV/dz ẑ.",
        "Differentiate √(R² + z²) − |z|: z/√(R² + z²) − z/|z|."
      ],
      "answer": {
        "text": "E_z(z) = (σ/2ε₀)(z/|z| − z/√(R² + z²))",
        "expression": "sigma/(2*eps0)*(z/abs(z)-z/sqrt(R^2+z^2))"
      },
      "uses": [
        "I.1"
      ],
      "followUps": [
        "Why does only E_z survive on the axis?",
        "How is E related to V?",
        "What does E look like far away?"
      ],
      "cards": [
        {
          "title": "Field from the potential",
          "note": "On the axis only E_z survives",
          "latex": "E_z = -\\frac{dV}{dz}",
          "branches": []
        },
        {
          "title": "Differentiate",
          "note": null,
          "latex": "\\frac{d}{dz}\\left(\\color{accent}{\\sqrt{R^2+z^2}} - \\color{second}{|z|}\\right) = \\color{accent}{\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{R^2+z^2}}} - \\color{second}{\\frac{z}{|z|}}",
          "branches": [
            {
              "title": "Above (z > 0)",
              "latex": "E_z = \\frac{\\sigma}{2\\varepsilon_0}\\left(1 - \\frac{z}{\\sqrt{R^2+z^2}}\\right)"
            },
            {
              "title": "Below (z < 0)",
              "latex": "E_z = -\\frac{\\sigma}{2\\varepsilon_0}\\left(1 + \\frac{z}{\\sqrt{R^2+z^2}}\\right)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answerLatex": "E_z(z) = \\frac{\\sigma}{2\\varepsilon_0}\\left(\\frac{z}{|z|} - \\frac{z}{\\sqrt{R^2+z^2}}\\right)"
    },
    {
      "label": "I.3",
      "asks": "The jump of E_z across the disc, and sketches of E_z and V.",
      "hint": "Compare E_z just above and just below the disc (z → 0⁺ and z → 0⁻).",
      "steps": [
        "Just above, E_z → σ/2ε₀; just below, E_z → −σ/2ε₀.",
        "The jump is σ/ε₀, as for any charged surface; V is continuous."
      ],
      "answer": {
        "text": "ΔE_z = σ/ε₀, V continuous",
        "expression": "sigma/eps0"
      },
      "uses": [
        "I.2"
      ],
      "followUps": [
        "Why does E_z jump across the disc?",
        "Is V continuous at z = 0?",
        "How does this compare with an infinite plane?"
      ]
    }
  ],
